<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The boss of the air traffic control provid=
er Nats has been summoned by the UK transport secretary, as flight chaos ca=
rried on into a second day after a system problem, with more than 1,300 fli=
ghts cancelled to and from UK airports.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Nats provides air traffic control services=
 to 15 UK airports. Its chief executive, Martin Rolfe, is due to speak to H=
eidi Alexander before she addresses MPs in the Commons for the second readi=
ng of the civil aviation bill.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Tens of thousands of passengers have been =
caught up in the chaos, with more cancellations and delays expected as airl=
ines deal with the knock-on effects at airports including Heathrow, Gatwick=
, Manchester and Stansted.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">According to FlightRadar, 246 flights had =
been cancelled at Heathrow airport by 6am today, while Gatwick passengers f=
aced 33 cancellations.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Rolfe said he =E2=80=9Calways accepts full=
 responsibility=E2=80=9D when asked if he was considering his position afte=
r the incident.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">He told Sky News: =E2=80=9CI am the CEO of=
 a piece of critical infrastructure, so it is my job to take responsibility=
 for that.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CWhat I absolutely take responsibi=
lity for is keeping people safe and delivering the best service we possibly=
 can on a day like today.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CYou will understand my focus is e=
ntirely about how we actually resolve this issue, get things back to normal=
, help our airlines, our airport customers and the travelling public.=E2=80=
=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The =E2=80=9Ctechnical issue=E2=80=9D at t=
he air traffic control services provider Nats caused Britain=E2=80=99s thir=
d major stoppage in just over three years.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The breakdown started at about 1pm on Tues=
day and affected departing flights at many of Britain=E2=80=99s biggest air=
ports including Heathrow, Gatwick, Manchester and Stansted. By the time Nat=
s said it had fixed its flight-processing system almost four hours later, m=
ost flights scheduled to depart were yet to take off. The disruption spread=
 to include incoming flights to much of southern England.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">In the markets, oil prices are rising towa=
rds the $100 a barrel level for the first time since July.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Brent crude has jumped 1.5% to $99.36 a ba=
rrel, as escalating attacks across the Middle East are fuelling fears over =
supply disruptions.</p>
